{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Schenectady and Troy Railroad -- Troy's answer to being bypassed by the Mohawk and Hudson Railroad, which since 1831 had funneled Erie Canal-corridor traffic into Albany instead (see new_york_central.geojson). Incorporated May 21 1836; the city of Troy itself gained stock control in 1838, possibly the first municipally-controlled railroad in the United States. Construction began 1841 and trains began running Schenectady-Troy in November 1842 over a 20.91-mile (33.65 km) standard-gauge line up the north bank of the Mohawk, with operator-agents at Aqueduct, Niskayuna and Crescent, and further stops at Dunsbach Ferry, Cohoes and Green Island. The company never built its own Hudson River bridge, instead running into Troy on trackage rights over the Rensselaer and Saratoga Railroad's bridge (that R&S corridor is a separate feature in cities/albany_commuter_rail.geojson). It was one of the ten roads consolidated into the first New York Central Railroad on May 17 1853, after which the line was operated as NYC's Troy and Schenectady Branch. It survived as a through route until 1964, when the segment between Aqueduct and Crescent was abandoned so the state could eliminate an awkward grade crossing with the Adirondack Northway (removal in 1965); both ends stayed in local freight use, the west end going to Conrail in 1976 and the east end being run for a time by the Delaware and Hudson under subsidy. The right-of-way is now the Mohawk-Hudson Bike-Hike Trail. Geometry is the digitized right-of-way from Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0), not a waypoint approximation.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schenectady_and_Troy_Railroad (incorporated May 21 1836 per ICC 1930 p.607; Troy stock control 1838 per Klein 1985 p.18 and Stevens 1926 pp.240-241; construction began 1841, trains running November 1842; 20.91 mi; standard gauge; trackage rights over the Rensselaer and Saratoga's Hudson bridge per Stevens 1926 pp.245-246; consolidated into the first New York Central May 17 1853 per ICC 1930 pp.211-212; through route until 1964 abandonment Aqueduct-Crescent, removal 1965; west end to Conrail 1976; east end Crescent-Green Island operated by the D&H under subsidy; right-of-way now the Mohawk-Hudson Bike-Hike Trail)", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Schenectady_and_Troy_Railroad.map (CC BY 4.0) -- digitized right-of-way geometry used for both features here", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/troy-schenectady-railroad/ (four passenger trains each way daily Schenectady-Troy in 1913; Crescent-Cohoes 2.3 mi, Cohoes-Green Island 2.9 mi; later known as the NYC Troy Branch, then the Penn Central / Conrail Aqueduct Branch)"], "not_yet_sourced": "The date passenger service actually ended on the Troy and Schenectady Branch. The 1964 end date used here is the abandonment of the Aqueduct-Crescent segment, i.e. the end of the through route, NOT a documented last passenger train -- passenger service very likely ended earlier, but no source for the date has been found. Frequencies before and after the documented 1913 figure of four trains each way daily are estimated. Also unsourced: 1840s-era running times, the exact Troy-side alignment over the Rensselaer and Saratoga bridge, and whether the branch carried any named trains.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Schenectady and Troy Railroad", "line": "Schenectady and Troy: Schenectady and Troy Railroad", "id": "schenectady-troy-railroad-independent", "color": "#0E7C7B", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 1840s steam on a 20.91-mile line with six intermediate stops; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1842, "end": 1853, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Trains began running Schenectady-Troy in November 1842; built and stock-controlled by the city of Troy to counter Albany's Mohawk and Hudson connection; entered Troy on trackage rights over the Rensselaer and Saratoga's Hudson River bridge; frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schenectady_and_Troy_Railroad", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Schenectady_and_Troy_Railroad.map", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/troy-schenectady-railroad/"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-73.94176, 42.81583], [-73.9415, 42.81616], [-73.94114, 42.81663], [-73.94066, 42.81701], [-73.93936, 42.8177], [-73.93784, 42.81822], [-73.93753, 42.81838], [-73.93734, 42.81851], [-73.93718, 42.81856], [-73.93706, 42.81861], [-73.93688, 42.81867], [-73.93487, 42.81954], [-73.93425, 42.8198], [-73.93348, 42.82015], [-73.9325, 42.82062], [-73.93218, 42.82082], [-73.9317, 42.8211], [-73.93114, 42.82152], [-73.93065, 42.82194], [-73.92991, 42.82264], [-73.92985, 42.82272], [-73.92981, 42.82277], [-73.9297, 42.8229], [-73.92927, 42.82349], [-73.92887, 42.82414], [-73.92818, 42.82533], [-73.92737, 42.8267], [-73.9269, 42.82753], [-73.92658, 42.82808], [-73.92551, 42.82991], [-73.92468, 42.83134], [-73.92429, 42.83196], [-73.92419, 42.8321], [-73.92415, 42.83216], [-73.92414, 42.8322], [-73.92413, 42.83228], [-73.9241, 42.83238], [-73.92356, 42.83324], [-73.9231, 42.83398], [-73.92279, 42.83441], [-73.92231, 42.83499], [-73.92177, 42.83549], [-